{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLKE84FwYUQRKfuFHUz3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Mathematica code printer now prints derivatives as `D[...]` and writes float exponents in Mathematica's `*^` notation. I added the two methods from the issue to `MCodePrinter` in `sympy/printing/mathematica.py`, with one change: `_print_Float` builds on the inherited float printer instead of calling `str(expr)`, then swaps `e` for `*^`.\n\nWhat it prints now:\n- `Derivative(f(t), t)` → `D[f[t], t]`\n- `Derivative(sin(x)*f(t), x, t, x)` → `D[f[t]*Sin[x], x, t, x]`\n- `Float('1.0e-20')` → `1.00000000000000*^-20`\n\nTwo things to know:\n- **The issue's own example doesn't show the change.** The printer writes `1.0e-4` as `0.000100000000000000`, which has no exponent to convert. The `*^` form only appears for very small or very large numbers, like the `1.0e-20` above.\n- **Large exponents keep a plus sign.** `9.4e30` becomes `9.40000000000000*^+30`. I believe Mathematica accepts the `+`, but I haven't checked it in Mathematica itself.\n\nThe 9 existing tests in `sympy/printing/tests/test_mathematica.py` pass; none of them cover derivatives or floats, so the outputs above come from running the printer directly."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":511,"cache_read_input_tokens":20353,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":511},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c565582-3bcc-424c-9ee4-1645fa4af4b0","uuid":"9e351559-de93-4824-b94d-85d22cf2dc27","timestamp":"2026-11-23T18:14:47.718Z","request_id":"req_011ChLKE7u5DoUVPRMR1z7VE"}
